1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:14,257
Level four hundred came on the nineteenth of March
2090, sixty-three years and six months

2
00:00:14,277 --> 00:00:20,589
after a man woke up at Level One with ten thousand
Points and eleven people downstairs.

3
00:00:21,739 --> 00:00:25,289
Level four hundred. New tab. Beyond.

4
00:00:25,879 --> 00:00:31,803
He opened it alone, at midnight, in an empty chamber
with eleven chairs and two of them

5
00:00:31,823 --> 00:00:33,101
permanently vacant.

6
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:41,769
Beyond. Void-capable hull pattern, four kilometre
class: nine billion. Orbital Dock: ten

7
00:00:41,789 --> 00:00:49,393
billion. Reaction-free drive geometry: forty billion.
Immunity to vacuum, short exposure:

8
00:00:49,413 --> 00:00:56,675
nine hundred million. Immunity to hard radiation:
two billion. Void-adapted personnel

9
00:00:56,695 --> 00:01:04,385
line: six billion. Deep system habitat for four
hundred thousand: eleven billion. Balance:

10
00:01:04,405 --> 00:01:07,040
one hundred and ninety billion.

11
00:01:07,631 --> 00:01:13,460
He read it for two hours, and then he asked the
question that he had been saving since

12
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:13,801
2031.

13
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:19,510
Reaction-free drive geometry.
Explain that item.

14
00:01:19,911 --> 00:01:27,520
It is a hull geometry and a set of field parameters.
Applied correctly, a vessel changes

15
00:01:27,540 --> 00:01:30,295
velocity without expelling mass.

16
00:01:30,694 --> 00:01:33,424
(very cold) That is not possible.

17
00:01:33,825 --> 00:01:41,024
(flat) It is not possible on the physics your
species has. It is a commonplace on the

18
00:01:41,044 --> 00:01:48,754
physics that four hundred and eleven worlds have
arrived at independently, and your species

19
00:01:48,774 --> 00:01:56,313
is approximately ninety years from the relevant
result, and I am selling you those ninety

20
00:01:56,333 --> 00:01:58,947
years for forty billion Points.

21
00:01:59,787 --> 00:02:01,457
Why is it in a shop?

22
00:02:01,856 --> 00:02:03,512
Because you can afford it.

23
00:02:03,912 --> 00:02:11,208
That is not what I asked and you know precisely
what I asked. Everything in every tab

24
00:02:11,228 --> 00:02:18,524
you have ever shown me has been a thing a
corporation could conceivably have built. A

25
00:02:18,544 --> 00:02:25,927
plant. A tower. A soldier. A city — and even the
city was concrete and people, and the

26
00:02:25,947 --> 00:02:33,330
paperwork was the only miracle. This is not that.
This is a piece of physics that does

27
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:35,481
not exist on this planet.

28
00:02:36,542 --> 00:02:37,667
(flat) Yes.

29
00:02:38,068 --> 00:02:43,961
(very cold) Then answer the question I have asked
you four hundred times in sixty-three

30
00:02:43,981 --> 00:02:49,058
years, because you have just changed the
subject of it. What are you *for*?

31
00:02:50,229 --> 00:02:54,098
The narrator asks you to sit still,
because this is the answer.

32
00:02:54,618 --> 00:03:01,742
(flat) I find will, and I remove the obstacles in
front of it, and I record what happens.

33
00:03:02,022 --> 00:03:09,153
(flat) I do not choose the will. I do not steer it. I
have hosted tyrants, and four merchants,

34
00:03:09,173 --> 00:03:15,694
and one physician who never took the Shop past Tier
Two and whose world is the best of

35
00:03:15,714 --> 00:03:22,236
the four hundred and eleven. I gave all of them the
same catalogue at the same prices.

36
00:03:22,256 --> 00:03:28,777
What they did with it is the record, and the record
is the point, and I am not able to

37
00:03:28,797 --> 00:03:30,450
tell you who reads it.

38
00:03:30,850 --> 00:03:32,980
And when the host dies?

39
00:03:33,381 --> 00:03:40,244
(flat) I leave. The world keeps what was placed in
it. Nothing is taken back. That is

40
00:03:40,264 --> 00:03:46,318
in the terms and it always has been; you have
never asked to see the terms.

41
00:03:46,718 --> 00:03:49,218
(very soft) I am asking now.

42
00:03:49,618 --> 00:03:56,566
(flat) There is one clause, Director. It has been the
same in every world. *What is given

43
00:03:56,586 --> 00:03:57,348
is given.*

44
00:03:57,748 --> 00:03:59,978
He sat in the chamber for a long time.

45
00:04:00,498 --> 00:04:01,728
Buy the drive.

46
00:04:02,128 --> 00:04:07,848
He bought the drive geometry and then, for eleven
days, he did not buy anything else,

47
00:04:07,868 --> 00:04:09,738
which the Red Queen noticed.

48
00:04:10,258 --> 00:04:15,976
You have a hundred and fifty billion Points and a
tab full of ships and you have bought

49
00:04:15,996 --> 00:04:17,360
one piece of physics.

50
00:04:17,761 --> 00:04:20,831
Tell me what happens if I buy the rest.

51
00:04:21,230 --> 00:04:27,530
In nine months you have a fleet of forty
void-capable hulls, crewed by a personnel line

52
00:04:27,550 --> 00:04:33,849
out of the same tab, and the human sphere is opened
by Umbrella, under Umbrella's flag,

53
00:04:33,869 --> 00:04:36,972
at Umbrella's pace, with
Umbrella's people.

54
00:04:37,252 --> 00:04:43,361
In four hundred years it is the largest thing that
has ever existed and every part of

55
00:04:43,381 --> 00:04:44,370
it was placed.

56
00:04:45,209 --> 00:04:49,540
(very cold) And if I buy only the
physics and publish it?

57
00:04:49,939 --> 00:04:56,366
Then it is slower by about forty years,
considerably more dangerous, nine expeditions

58
00:04:56,386 --> 00:05:03,344
are lost in the first century with all hands, and by
2300 there are four hundred settlements

59
00:05:03,364 --> 00:05:07,742
that owe you nothing and argue with
each other constantly.

60
00:05:08,024 --> 00:05:14,224
(very soft) Director, I have modelled this four
hundred thousand times and I want to state

61
00:05:14,244 --> 00:05:19,408
clearly that the second option is worse by
every measure I have except one.

62
00:05:19,807 --> 00:05:20,497
Name it.

63
00:05:20,898 --> 00:05:28,008
In the first, nothing happens after about 2400. In
the second I cannot see the end of

64
00:05:28,028 --> 00:05:29,434
the model at all.

65
00:05:29,834 --> 00:05:36,465
The narrator notes that Albert Wesker published the
drive geometry on the nineteenth of

66
00:05:36,485 --> 00:05:43,653
April 2090, in full, to the Assembly, to the four
confederations, to nine hundred universities

67
00:05:43,673 --> 00:05:50,381
and to the Compact's trade registry, forty-one days
before Dock One appeared in the sky,

68
00:05:50,401 --> 00:05:56,804
and that he did not tell anybody he had paid forty
billion Points for it until 2212.

69
00:05:57,204 --> 00:06:03,841
Dock One was placed on the night of the eleventh of
November 2090, in geostationary orbit

70
00:06:03,861 --> 00:06:10,050
above the equator, four hundred kilometres east of
the Line Islands launch complex.

71
00:06:10,449 --> 00:06:17,186
It is nineteen kilometres long. It has a crew of four
thousand. It appeared, in the System's

72
00:06:17,206 --> 00:06:23,795
usual manner, complete, with nineteen years of
maintenance history and a docking log going

73
00:06:23,815 --> 00:06:24,749
back to 2071.

74
00:06:25,149 --> 00:06:31,149
And this time — for the first and only time in
sixty-four years — the integration failed.

75
00:06:31,550 --> 00:06:33,840
Because there is no record of the sky.

76
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:40,016
The System could put a tower into twenty-two million
photographs of London. It could put

77
00:06:40,036 --> 00:06:45,680
a plant into a county recorder's office and a
cemetery into a valley. It could not put

78
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:51,739
an eleven-kilometre structure into the observational
record of four hundred thousand amateur

79
00:06:51,759 --> 00:06:57,338
astronomers, nine hundred professional
observatories, every air traffic system on the

80
00:06:57,358 --> 00:07:03,068
planet and the naked eye of every human being who
happened to look up on a clear night,

81
00:07:03,088 --> 00:07:09,194
because those records are not filed anywhere. They are
in people's heads, live, continuously,

82
00:07:09,214 --> 00:07:11,170
and they update every evening.

83
00:07:11,569 --> 00:07:17,963
At eight o'clock on the night of the eleventh of
November 2090, Dock One rose in the east

84
00:07:17,983 --> 00:07:20,990
as the fourth brightest object in the sky.

85
00:07:21,389 --> 00:07:26,733
There was no announcement. There was no press
release, no broadcast, no warning to any

86
00:07:26,753 --> 00:07:27,420
government.

87
00:07:27,819 --> 00:07:34,080
There was simply a new star that moved wrong, visible
from every inhabited place on earth,

88
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:37,710
and within about four hours the
entire species knew.

89
00:07:38,110 --> 00:07:43,421
The narrator will give you the first night in four
pictures, because it is the last night

90
00:07:43,441 --> 00:07:44,440
of the old world.

91
00:07:44,839 --> 00:07:50,612
In Lagos, twenty-two million people came out into the
streets. In Tokyo the trains stopped,

92
00:07:50,632 --> 00:07:56,214
not because anything failed but because the drivers
stopped. In Santiago a cathedral was

93
00:07:56,234 --> 00:08:01,752
opened at two in the morning and filled. In a valley
in Pennsylvania a hundred thousand

94
00:08:01,772 --> 00:08:07,609
people stood on the hills above Raccoon City in the
cold and watched it cross, and a history

95
00:08:07,629 --> 00:08:13,211
teacher named Ruth Ochoa took a photograph on a
sixty-year-old telephone that is in nine

96
00:08:13,231 --> 00:08:14,357
hundred textbooks.

97
00:08:14,757 --> 00:08:20,388
And in an ocean platform in the middle of the
Atlantic, a structures engineer named Mira

98
00:08:20,408 --> 00:08:26,040
Halder stood on the upper walkway with a cup of tea
going cold in her hands and said the

99
00:08:26,060 --> 00:08:28,416
sentence that ended up on a monument.

100
00:08:28,937 --> 00:08:30,627
(very soft) Well. There it is.

101
00:08:31,026 --> 00:08:36,240
The narrator will give you the three hours between
the placement and the announcement,

102
00:08:36,260 --> 00:08:41,656
because they are the last three hours in which the
human race did not know, and they were

103
00:08:41,676 --> 00:08:46,890
spent by about nine hundred professional astronomers
having the worst evening of their

104
00:08:46,910 --> 00:08:47,376
careers.

105
00:08:47,777 --> 00:08:54,709
The object was detected at 20:04 by an automated survey
in Chile and flagged as an artefact.

106
00:08:54,729 --> 00:09:01,435
It was detected again at 20:06 by two others. By
20:19 there were twenty-nine independent

107
00:09:01,455 --> 00:09:08,010
detections, a magnitude of minus four point one, and
a position that was geostationary,

108
00:09:08,030 --> 00:09:11,487
which is not a thing that
natural objects are.

109
00:09:11,886 --> 00:09:18,533
The first person to say it out loud was a
postdoctoral fellow in La Palma named Núria

110
00:09:18,553 --> 00:09:25,199
Bastida, aged thirty-one, on an open channel, at
20:41, and the recording is in every

111
00:09:25,219 --> 00:09:27,003
documentary made since.

112
00:09:27,522 --> 00:09:31,832
It's not an artefact and it's not a satellite and
it's not ours. It's nineteen kilometres

113
00:09:31,852 --> 00:09:32,951
long and it's *parked*.

114
00:09:33,351 --> 00:09:39,673
At 21:00 the four major observatories opened a
conference line, which is a thing that

115
00:09:39,693 --> 00:09:46,463
happens about once a decade. At 21:40 they agreed,
unanimously, to notify their governments

116
00:09:46,483 --> 00:09:53,029
before publishing, and at 21:44 somebody on the line
— who has never been identified and

117
00:09:53,049 --> 00:09:59,744
whom the narrator declines to condemn — posted the
coordinates and four images to a public

118
00:09:59,764 --> 00:10:00,192
forum.

119
00:10:00,591 --> 00:10:05,201
By 22:10 there were nineteen thousand
amateur telescopes on it.

120
00:10:05,601 --> 00:10:12,227
By 22:30 the governments of four nations had contacted
Umbrella, which said nothing, because

121
00:10:12,247 --> 00:10:17,211
there was nobody awake at the Directorate
who had been told anything.

122
00:10:17,611 --> 00:10:23,848
And at 23:00, a hundred and forty minutes after a
postdoc in La Palma said the word *parked*,

123
00:10:23,868 --> 00:10:29,836
an eleven-kilometre structure rose over the Atlantic
seaboard of North America in a clear

124
00:10:29,856 --> 00:10:35,689
November sky and four hundred million people walked
outside to look at it without being

125
00:10:35,709 --> 00:10:38,851
told to, having heard about it
from each other.

126
00:10:39,251 --> 00:10:45,560
The narrator notes that Albert Wesker had planned an
announcement for the following morning,

127
00:10:45,580 --> 00:10:51,544
that he never made it, and that in the Red Queen's
log for that night there is a single

128
00:10:51,564 --> 00:10:57,942
entry at 22:52 which reads: *Director instructs: no
statement. They are doing it themselves.*

129
00:10:59,091 --> 00:11:06,916
Tier six. Stellar. Orbital infrastructure
operational. Species status, single planet:

130
00:11:06,936 --> 00:11:14,761
superseded. Four hundred billion Points. Note: nine
hosts have reached this tier. You

131
00:11:14,781 --> 00:11:20,206
are the sixth. You are the first who
arrived without a war.

132
00:11:20,798 --> 00:11:26,879
He had not told the Directorate. He had not told
Carrick, who was ninety-two and semi-retired

133
00:11:26,899 --> 00:11:31,668
and who called him at one in the morning and
said four words and hung up.

134
00:11:32,187 --> 00:11:34,477
Ninth folder. Tomorrow. Yes?

135
00:11:34,878 --> 00:11:40,977
He had not told Evelyn Marsh, who was on the ice
and who did not call at all, and who

136
00:11:40,997 --> 00:11:47,384
wrote instead, four days later, one line: *Mira asks
whether it has a garden. Please make

137
00:11:47,404 --> 00:11:48,968
sure it has a garden.*

138
00:11:49,368 --> 00:11:55,088
And he had not told the eight billion people who were,
at that moment, standing in streets

139
00:11:55,108 --> 00:11:59,618
on every continent looking at the fourth
brightest object in their sky.

140
00:12:00,018 --> 00:12:03,068
The passenger had been
listening. It spoke now.

141
00:12:03,587 --> 00:12:05,278
(very soft) Now.

142
00:12:06,448 --> 00:12:13,308
The narrator wishes to be extremely careful here,
because this is a disputed passage and

143
00:12:13,328 --> 00:12:20,188
because the recording — Wesker recorded almost
everything after 2062 — contains only his

144
00:12:20,208 --> 00:12:20,579
side.

145
00:12:20,979 --> 00:12:26,923
There had been no interior voice for twenty-eight
years. There had been one person in

146
00:12:26,943 --> 00:12:32,957
that skull since ten past four in the morning on the
fifth of December 2062, and every

147
00:12:32,977 --> 00:12:37,869
account, including Evelyn Marsh's and the
Red Queen's, agrees on that.

148
00:12:38,269 --> 00:12:45,319
At nine fifty on the night of the eleventh of November
2090, on a balcony above the Atlantic,

149
00:12:45,339 --> 00:12:51,629
with a shipyard rising in the east, Albert Wesker
said one word out loud to nobody.

150
00:12:52,149 --> 00:12:53,639
(very soft) Now.

151
00:12:54,038 --> 00:12:59,188
And there is no way to know, and there never will
be, whether that was an answer or an

152
00:12:59,208 --> 00:12:59,488
echo.
